---
layout: article
title: Start with Videos
description: Create your first Appwrite video from a Storage file, encode an adaptive ladder, and play the stream in a browser.
difficulty: beginner
readtime: 5
---

In this quick start you create a video from a file, encode it into a few renditions, and play the stream with a web player.

# Create encoding profiles {% #create-encoding-profiles %}

New projects have no [encoding profiles](/docs/products/videos/profiles), so create a small ladder first. You only do this once per project.

1. Sign in to [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io) and open a project.
2. Open **Videos** in the sidebar and select **Encoding profiles**.
3. Click **Create profile**, choose the **360p** preset, and click **Create**.
4. Repeat for the **720p** and **1080p** presets.

# Create a video {% #create-a-video %}

1. Select **Videos** and click **Create video**.
2. Choose a source:
   - **Upload**: Drop an MP4, MOV, WebM, MKV, or audio file and pick a **Destination bucket**. If the project has no buckets yet, click **Create Videos bucket**. The uploaded file is stored in that bucket and stays there.
   - **Storage**: Choose a file that is already in a bucket. Nothing is copied.
   - **Demo**: Import an open movie to explore Videos without a file of your own.
3. Optionally enter a **Name**. It defaults to the file name.
4. Under **Profiles**, keep the selection or change it. Every profile that would not upscale the source is selected for you. Profiles larger than the source are marked **Upscale**, since upscaling adds size without adding detail.
5. Keep the output at **CMAF**, the default. CMAF encodes once and serves both an HLS and a DASH manifest. To pick HLS or DASH instead, open **Advanced settings**.
6. Click **Create video**.

Video creation continues in the background, so you can close the wizard while the file uploads and encodes.

# Watch it encode {% #watch-it-encode %}

Open the video and select **Renditions**. Each rendition moves from **Queued** to **Ready**, with a progress percentage while it encodes.

The video page shows a player as soon as one rendition is ready.

# Allow playback {% #allow-playback %}

A video created in the Console has no permissions yet, so it only plays inside the Console. To play it from a public page, grant **Read** to **Any** on the video with a [server SDK](/docs/sdks#server):

```js
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const videos = new sdk.Videos(client);

await videos.update({
    videoId: '<VIDEO_ID>',
    name: '<VIDEO_NAME>', // required, pass the current name to keep it
    permissions: [sdk.Permission.read(sdk.Role.any())]
});
```

The source file in Storage can stay private. Playback checks the video's permissions, not the file's. See [Permissions](/docs/products/videos/permissions) to keep videos private to signed-in users or teams.

# Play the stream {% #play-the-stream %}

Select **Install** on the video page. Pick a platform, player, and format, then copy the install steps and code. For a web page with [hls.js](https://github.com/video-dev/hls.js):

```sh
npm install hls.js
```

```js
import Hls from 'hls.js'

const video = document.getElementById('player')
const src =
  'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/videos/<VIDEO_ID>/outputs/cmaf/master.m3u8?project=<PROJECT_ID>'

if (Hls.isSupported()) {
  const hls = new Hls()
  hls.loadSource(src)
  hls.attachMedia(video)
} else if (video.canPlayType('application/vnd.apple.mpegurl')) {
  // Safari plays HLS natively
  video.src = src
}
```

```html
<video id="player" controls playsinline></video>
<script type="module" src="/main.js"></script>
```

The player loads the master manifest and picks the best rendition for the viewer's connection.

